think Second lap of the run. The scrubber proved timed geometry and the timecode proved timed text, each alone; the higher-Lindy next move is to prove they COMPOSE, since a real video player shows both at once -- a capstone that demonstrates the family's pieces stand together, worth more than another isolated primitive.
think Chose compose-don't-braid: image/player_hud.rye calls scrubber.paint_scrubber and timecode.paint_timecode -- the very verbs their own witnesses proved -- over one shared background, adding only the composition and the disjointness check. No new storage, codec, palette, glyph, or font.
think Designed the crux as CLEAN composition: prove the two overlay rectangles are disjoint before a pixel is painted (rects_disjoint, refusing HudOverlaysOverlap by name), so the paint order cannot matter; then prove each overlay keeps its own invariant under composition and the HUD touches only the union of the two rects.
think Placed the moving block in the clear mid-band between the upper-left badge and the lower-edge bar, so motion is always visible outside both overlays and the touches-only-its-overlays check is meaningful.
obs GREEN on the first metal run: the two overlays disjoint (a bar clashing the badge refused HudOverlaysOverlap), the HUD touching only the badge and the bar; scrubber fill grows 0->328 px empty->full, timecode 1 tick + 22 steady pairs -- each keeping its own invariant; round-trip byte for byte through both codecs.
obs tame_style_check caught four compound asserts (rectangle-extent and canvas-match pairs); split each so the failing half is named, then GREEN. The gfd1 adaptive encoder chose 0 deltas + 24 keyframes (block, fill, and clock all move every frame), so gfd1 9143 bytes is a hair over gfv1 9119 -- reported not claimed (REDS #82).
